.TH SBX-TRACE 1 "" "sbx-trace 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sbx-trace \- show which files a program opened, looked for or changed, and which attempts failed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sbx-trace
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-host\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.br
.B sbx-trace \-\-last
[\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B sbx-trace \-\-suggest
[\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B sbx-trace \-\-diff
[\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B sbx-trace \-\-html
[\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fINAME\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sbx-trace
is a tool apart from
.BR sbx .
It starts a command and watches it from outside with
.BR bpftrace (8):
the system calls that open, look at, run or change a path, for the command and everything it starts. The command is held until the probes are in place. Without
.B \-\-host
only processes inside a new pid namespace are kept, which leaves out bubblewrap's own setup work and shows what the sandboxed program did.
.PP
It needs
.BR bpftrace ,
a kernel with BTF (\fI/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux\fR) and root: it asks through
.BR sudo
on a terminal, else
.BR pkexec .
Run it as your own user, not as root. The exit code is the command's own.
.PP
The report lists, for each path, how many times it was read (r), written or changed (w), looked at without opening (p: stat, access, readlink) or run (x), and the errors. At the top, \fIblocked\fR lists the paths the program was refused (denied, read-only) or, in a sandbox, could not find although the host has them (hidden). A hidden path is often just what the sandbox is for.
.PP
Paths are grouped into your home and the system. \fI/proc\fR and \fI/sys\fR are never shown; \fI/dev\fR and \fI/run\fR only when an attempt failed. A path that is relative to a directory descriptor, or very long, can't be told from outside and is only counted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report (or suggestions, or comparison) as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-host
Keep every process, not only those in a new pid namespace. Use it to trace a program that is not sandboxed.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Rows to print per group (default 40).
.TP
.B \-\-last \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Show the last saved report again: that of NAME, or the newest.
.TP
.B \-\-suggest \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Print \fBread_only=\fR and \fBwhitelist=\fR lines for what the saved report shows was refused or hidden. Each is the path itself, never its folder, and read-only unless the program tried to write. Your home, Downloads and the like, sbx's own folders, anything that holds secrets (\fI~/.ssh\fR, \fI~/.gnupg\fR, keyrings...) and paths already shared are left out. Read each line before you use it.
.TP
.B \-\-diff \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Compare the last report with the one before it: which blocked paths no longer are (and whether they now work), which are newly blocked, and how many paths only one run touched. Use it to check that a setting helped.
.TP
.B \-\-html \fR[\fINAME\fR]
Write the last saved report as one web page you can open, keep or send: a map of the folders (sized by the number of paths, coloured by the most serious thing in each: written, hidden, read-only, denied, or a place that holds secrets that the program reached), filters, a search and the list of what needs attention. The page is a single file that needs no network; your home shows as \fI~\fR. Without \fB\-o\fR it goes to the standard output.
.TP
.B \-o \fIFILE\fR, \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
Where \fB\-\-html\fR writes the page. Put it before NAME.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sbx-trace/NAME.json
The last report of NAME, which is the program (for \fBsbx\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR, the program sbx runs). The one before it is in \fIprevious/\fR. Both are private to you.
.SH NOTES
Besides the calls that open and look at a path, writes (unlink, rename, mkdir, chmod, truncate...) are watched. If bpftrace refuses that larger set, which can happen on a kernel with other system calls, the trace starts again with the basic set, which sees no writes and does not read the flags of openat2 (so a write made through it counts as a read), and says so; the command has not run yet at that point.
.PP
If bpftrace reports lost events the report says it is incomplete. Unix sockets and network connections are not traced.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sbx (1),
.BR bpftrace (8)
